response_text:
Below is a complete, self-contained Rust project that ports the provided TypeScript LRU cache to idiomatic Rust using Tokio. It provides:

- TTL-based expiration
- LRU eviction when max entries is exceeded
- Memory-based eviction (using a simple size estimate)
- Weak reference bookkeeping for values stored as Arc<T> (so if the underlying Arc is dropped elsewhere, the cache can detect and evict)
- An async eviction loop driven by Tokio
- Tests that mirror the TypeScript tests (they run under Tokio and exercise the same behaviors)

Notes about the implementation choices:
- To make the cache usable from async tasks & an async eviction loop, the cache internals are protected with tokio::sync::Mutex and the public API is async.
- Values are stored as Arc<V> internally, so get returns an Arc<V>. This avoids expensive cloning of the inner value; cloning an Arc is cheap (refcount bump).
- To support weak-ref cleanup, Arc<V> is upcast to Arc<dyn Any + Send + Sync> and a Weak to that trait object is maintained alongside the entry. This requires V: Any + Send + Sync + 'static.
- The "hot path" (get/put) avoids cloning the inner V itself; only Arc clones are performed which are cheap. (If you need a different API that returns borrowed references without Arc clones, that would require holding a lock across the borrow and would be more complex for concurrency.)
- No unsafe code is used.

